# PATHS ARE ANCHORED WITH A LEADING SLASH ON PURPOSE.
#
# `evidence/` without the slash matches a directory of that name at ANY depth,
# so it silently excluded src/main/evidence/ as well as the intended capture
# dump. That directory holds fetch-raw.ts, which is the only network egress in
# the app and the enforcement point for Law 1 and Law 5. It was missing from
# the first commit, three files import it, and a clean clone could not compile.
# Nothing about that was visible in `git status`, because ignored files are
# exactly what git does not tell you about.
#
# Anchor every path that names a specific top-level directory or file.

/node_modules/
/dist/
/out/

# Raw captures of third-party sites, grouped by scan id. Never publishable.
/evidence/

# THE DATA ROOT. Everything the running app writes: the operator's API keys,
# client packets, raw captures and the approval ledger, plus Chromium's own
# profile files. It sits inside the checkout so an operator has one answer to
# "where are my files", which means the ignore rule below is the only thing
# standing between a `git add -A` and publishing a client's packet.
#
# The scrub guard in test-parsers.js walks src/, scripts/, docs/ and root-level
# markdown, so it does NOT scan this directory. Do not move client content out
# of here into a scanned path.
/data/

# Generated by scripts/copy-assets.js from the @fontsource packages. Keeping
# these out of the repo means the fonts stay an npm dependency rather than
# vendored redistribution, which removes the OFL-1.1 attribution question from
# an MIT release entirely. Run `npm run build:assets` to produce them.
#
# NOTE: this covers the SOURCE repo only. A packaged Electron build embeds the
# woff2 files, and redistributing them carries the OFL-1.1 notice requirement.
/assets/fonts/

# Same deal for vendored Leaflet (BSD-2): produced by `npm run build:assets`
# from the pinned npm package, never committed into this MIT repo.
/assets/vendor/

# Secrets. The real key store lives in %APPDATA%/assay/config.json, outside
# the repo entirely; these cover a stray copy landing here.
/.env
/.env.local
/config.json

# Local agent permissions, which carry absolute paths from whoever's machine
# wrote them. Previously excluded only by one developer's personal global git
# ignore, which is not a property of this repository and does not travel.
/.claude/

# Session memory. Also self-ignored via .remember/.gitignore; belt and braces.
/.remember/

# The operator's private list of client names, domains and handles that must
# never reach source or docs. Read by the scrub guard in test-parsers.js.
# It is untracked precisely because the old guard inlined these strings, so
# publishing the repo published the list it existed to protect.
/.scrub-terms

# INTERNAL WORKING DOCUMENTS. These stay on the operator's machine.
#
# Handoffs, audits, the build plan and the progress log describe how this
# operation is run: what is delivered, what is charged for, which client work
# informed which rubric anchor, and what is known to be broken and not yet
# said out loud. None of that belongs to someone who downloads the tool, and
# a reader cannot tell an internal note from documentation.
#
# They are still scanned by the scrub guard in test-parsers.js, which walks
# what is on disk rather than what git tracks, so they keep getting checked
# for client names even though they are no longer published.
# Globbed, not exact-named, so tomorrow's HANDOFF-next.md or AUDIT-2026-08-02.md
# is ignored the same as today's. Exact names left a gap a `git add -A` could
# walk a new internal doc straight through; preflight would still catch a leak,
# but the ignore is the first line and should not depend on remembering to
# rename in lockstep.
/HANDOFF*.md
/AUDIT*.md
/PLAN*.md
/PROGRESS*.md
/NOTES*.md
/docs/

*.log
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
